INV-lizor limb (a), the rest of it. Three root tests were left on
KNOWN_UNREACHABLE with the reason that a declaration could not honestly
express their subject. Re-read, that objection was aesthetic rather than
real: two have another TEST as their subject -- a standalone re-run of a
pinning module, and a shared harness -- and `# covers:` says exactly the
right thing about them, because "when this path changes, run me" is as
true of a test file as of a source. The third pins four package modules by
import path and can simply name them.

KNOWN_UNREACHABLE is now the empty set, which is a CLAIM: every root test
is selected by some change that can break it. It is worth something only
because the two ratchet arms fail when it stops being true.

THE HEADER SCAN NOW PARSES INSTEAD OF PATTERN-MATCHING, and this is the
defect the first two markers found. `_module_header` cut at the first line
beginning "import " -- which in test_rct_public_api_pinned.py is a line of
ENGLISH inside the module docstring ("the variants attach by / import path
+ signature"). The marker sat below the cut, the declaration silently did
nothing, and the ratchet reported the TEST as unreachable rather than the
parser as wrong, which is the expensive kind of wrong: it names the
innocent party. ast knows where a docstring ends; a line-start pattern
cannot. The regex survives as the fallback for a file that does not parse,
where over-scanning beats reading no header at all.

Verified by mutation: forcing the fallback path turns three tests red,
including the one that pins this exact docstring shape.

